---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeKitchenConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitsLifeKitchenConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="oz-to-cups">
  <UnitsLifeKitchenConverter client:load mode="oz-to-cups" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>&quot;Ounces&quot; means two different things</h2>
    <p>
      For oz to cups, the answer depends on what your recipe means, so this page gives both answers at
      once.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Fluid ounces measure volume. Eight of them make one US cup, by definition. Milk, water, stock,
        juice and oil in American recipes are almost always fluid ounces.
      </li>
      <li>
        Weight ounces measure mass: 28.35 g each. Flour, sugar, butter, chocolate and cheese in British
        and professional recipes are weight ounces, and the number of cups depends on the ingredient.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Eight ounces of water is a cup both ways, because for water the two readings are almost the same.
      Eight ounces of flour is one cup by volume and almost two by weight. The water case causes all
      the confusion.
    </p>

    <h2>How to tell which your recipe means</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Clue</th><th scope="col">Almost certainly</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>American recipe, liquid ingredient</td><td>Fluid ounces (volume)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>American recipe, solid ingredient</td><td>Weight ounces</td></tr>
        <tr><td>British recipe of any age</td><td>Weight ounces</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Written &quot;fl oz&quot;</td><td>Volume, explicitly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A can or packet size</td><td>What the label says. Check it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cheese, chocolate, pasta</td><td>Weight, always</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The eight-ounce cup, and where it breaks</h2>
    <p>
      "A cup is 8 ounces" is true for US fluid ounces and a US customary cup, and false for all others.
      A metric cup is 8.45 US fluid ounces. An imperial cup is 10 imperial fluid ounces. An 8 oz block
      of cream cheese is a weight, and it is about a cup only because cream cheese has about the density
      of water. That is a coincidence, not a rule.
    </p>

    <h2>Cans, cartons and the &quot;oz&quot; on the label</h2>
    <p>
      US packaging uses both. A 15 oz can of beans is a weight (425 g). A 12 fl oz can of soda is a
      volume (355 mL). A 14 fl oz can of coconut milk is a volume, and a 14 oz can of condensed milk is
      a weight. The letters "fl" are the only difference, and they are printed small.
    </p>

    <h2>The coffee cup is not a cup</h2>
    <p>
      Drip coffee makers measure in 5 fl oz "cups", a convention from the size of a teacup. So a
      twelve-cup carafe holds 60 fl oz: seven and a half real cups, or 1.77 litres. Coffee scoops are
      also their own unit, usually 2 tablespoons.
    </p>

    <h2>What this page computes</h2>
    <p>
      For the volume reading, ounces are multiplied by 29.5735 mL and divided by your chosen cup
      size. For the weight reading, ounces are multiplied by 28.349523125 g and divided by the grams
      per cup of the ingredient you selected. The working panel shows both calculations with your
      numbers, so you can see which assumption gave which answer.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
